<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Seuna Yadava dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1187 CE – 1317 CE · capital Devagiri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Deccan dynasty of Devagiri, whose wealth drew the first Delhi Sultanate raid into southern India in 1296.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Seunas were long-standing feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Chalukyas of Kalyani</a> in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> power collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhillama-v">Bhillama V</a> proclaimed independence around 1187, founded the fortress city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Devagiri</a> (later Daulatabad) and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> for control of the former Chalukya territories.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhana-ii">Singhana II</a>, the greatest ruler, extended Seuna power over most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. The court supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> literature: the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaskara-ii">Bhaskara</a>'s grandson ran a school under Seuna patronage, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemadri">Hemadri</a>, minister to Mahadeva and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramachandra-of-devagiri">Ramachandra</a>, wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaturvarga-chintamani">Chaturvarga Chintamani</a> and is credited with the Hemadpanti temple style. The poet-saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Jnaneshwar</a> wrote his Marathi commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Gita</a> in this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Devagiri</a> in 1296 and extracted vast tribute, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramachandra-of-devagiri">Ramachandra</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. After revolts, the kingdom was annexed in 1317 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-mubarak-shah">Mubarak Shah</a>. Devagiri, renamed Daulatabad, later served briefly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq's</a> capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/seuna-yadava-dynasty.jpg" alt="Seuna Yadava dynasty" width="320" height="149"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yadavas_of_Devagiri._Bhillama_V._1185-1193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1187 CE – 1317 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Devagiri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhillama V, Singhana II, Ramachandra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marathi, Kannada, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seunas, Yadavas of Devagiri, Seuna dynasty</dd></dl></aside></div>
